course: medicine
lesson: 11
title: Spots and itches: chickenpox, head lice and friends
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lots of common childhood spots and itches are caused by tiny living things: viruses, bacteria, a fungus, or small insects and worms.
explain: Chickenpox is a virus: red spots turn into blisters and then crust over in about a week, and it spreads very easily, so you stay home till all the spots have crusted.
explain: Head lice can't fly or jump; they walk from head to head when heads touch, and they like clean hair just as much as dirty hair.
explain: Several names lie: ringworm is a fungus, not a worm, and a cold sore has nothing to do with a cold.
explain: None of these mean someone is dirty, so nobody should be teased for them.
fact: Chickenpox is caused by the varicella-zoster virus; spots become blisters and crust over in about a week, and children stay home until all spots have crusted; it is usually mild, but some children get very ill with it.
fact: Ibuprofen should not be given for chickenpox unless a doctor says so, because it can sometimes lead to serious skin infections.
fact: After chickenpox you are usually immune (protected from catching it again), but the virus sleeps in the nerves and can wake years later as shingles, a painful rash.
fact: Many countries vaccinate against chickenpox, and the UK was reported to have added it to the routine childhood vaccines in 2026.
fact: Head lice spread by head-to-head contact, cannot fly or jump, lay eggs called nits on the hairs, and are found by wet-combing with conditioner and a fine nit comb.
fact: A verruca is a wart on the foot caused by a virus, often picked up from wet floors; it has no roots to dig out; in the UK children can usually swim with it covered.
fact: Ringworm is a fungus infection making a red scaly ring, treated with antifungal cream; it can come from people or pets.
fact: Threadworms are tiny white worms in the gut that come out at night to lay eggs, causing an itchy bottom; the whole family is treated.
fact: Cold sores are caused by a herpes simplex virus that stays in the body and can come back when you're ill or in the sun.
fact: Slapped cheek syndrome gives bright red cheeks, and by the time the rash appears you are no longer infectious.
fact: Hand, foot and mouth disease is a mild childhood virus with mouth ulcers and spots on the hands and feet, unrelated to the cattle disease.
fact: Impetigo is a bacterial skin infection with a crusty yellow rash, treated with antibacterial cream or antibiotics; it spreads easily, so children stay off school until it is treated.
fact: Scarlet fever is caused by streptococcus bacteria, gives a rough sandpaper rash, and is easily treated with antibiotics.
exercise: What causes chickenpox? | A virus | Bad food | Bee stings | Dirty hands only
exercise: When can a child with chickenpox go back to school? | When all the spots have crusted over | As soon as the very first spot appears | After one day, whatever the spots | When the itching stops for a bit
exercise: Which painkiller should not be given for chickenpox unless a doctor says so? | Ibuprofen | Paracetamol | Calamine lotion | Cooling gel
exercise: Can the chickenpox virus come back later? | Yes, it can wake years later as shingles | No, it is gone from the body completely after the spots | Yes, as chickenpox every year | No, it turns into a cold
exercise: How do head lice spread? | By head-to-head contact | By flying from one head to the next | By jumping across the room at school | From dirty hair only, never clean
exercise: How do you check for head lice? | Wet-comb with conditioner and a nit comb | Look with a bright torch from the other side of the room | Shave the whole head | Smell the hair for a funny smell
exercise: What is a verruca? | A wart on the foot caused by a virus | A splinter stuck deep in the heel | A blister from new shoes | A bee sting on the toe that has gone hard
exercise: What is ringworm? | A fungus infection of the skin | A small worm living under the skin | A bite from a worm | A kind of round bruise
exercise: Why does your bottom itch at night with threadworms? | The worms come out to lay eggs | The bed is too hot | You ate too many sweets before bed | Your pyjamas are scratchy and too tight
exercise: Do you get a cold sore from a cold? | No, it is caused by a herpes simplex virus | Yes, every cold gives you one | Yes, from going out in cold weather | No, it comes from having lots of icy cold drinks
exercise: Your cheeks look slapped red. Can you go to school? | Yes, by the time the rash shows you are not catching | No, stay home for a whole month | No, it spreads just by looking at someone | Only if you wear a mask all day
exercise: Is hand, foot and mouth disease the same as foot and mouth in cattle? | No, it is a different, mild childhood virus | Yes, you catch it from cows on farms | Yes, it is exactly the same germ | No, it comes from sharing shoes
exercise: What is impetigo? | A bacterial skin infection with crusty yellow sores | A kind of chickenpox that only grown-ups ever get | A sunburn on the lips | An allergy to soap and bubble bath
exercise: What gives scarlet fever its rash? | Streptococcus bacteria | A common cold virus | Eating too much red food | Sunburn on the chest and back
exercise: What do head lice lay on hairs? | Eggs called nits | Tiny worms | Little seeds | Sticky webs
exercise: Who is treated when a child has threadworms? | The whole family | Only the pet dog or cat | Only the child | Nobody, they go away alone in a day
test: letter home about nits, my mate says it's 'cos some kids are dirty. right? | No, lice like clean hair as much as dirty | Yes, only the dirty kids get them | Yes, they come from unwashed clothes and coats | No, they come from pigeons in the playground
test: In a cartoon, head lice leap from one kid's head across the classroom. What's wrong? | Lice can't jump or fly, they crawl | Nothing, lice are great jumpers | Lice only live on dogs and cats | Lice live in desks and come out at night
test: Mum says I'll get chickenpox only once, so let's go to a chickenpox party. Good idea? | No, some children get very ill from it | Yes, it's the best and safest way | Yes, the more spots the better for you | No, it gives you measles
test: Your friend's dog has a scaly patch and now he has a red ring on his arm. Likely? | Ringworm, a fungus that can come from pets | A real worm that crawled across from the dog | A bite mark | A burn from rubbing the dog's fur
test: Can you dig out a verruca by its roots? | No, verrucas don't have roots | Yes, the roots are deep like a plant's | Yes, with a needle | No, the roots are made of bone
test: Ella got a cold sore after a sunny holiday. Why now? | The virus sleeping in her body can come back in the sun | She caught a cold on the plane home | The sun creates the virus on the lips | She ate too much ice cream
test: Mia's crusty yellow sores spread when she scratches. What's the best plan? | See a GP or pharmacist for antibacterial treatment | Pick the crusts off so the air gets in | Hide them under make-up | Rub sand on them to dry them out
test: Grandad says scarlet fever is a deadly disease from olden days. What's true now? | It's easily treated with antibiotics | It's still always deadly | It can't be treated by anyone | It only ever happens to grown-ups
test: What would happen if only one person in a family with threadworms were treated? | The others could keep passing the eggs back | All the worms in the house would vanish | Nobody else could catch them | The pet would get them all instead
test: Which of these is NOT caused by a virus? | Ringworm | Chickenpox | Cold sores | Verrucas on the feet
test: Why is impetigo kept away from school until treated? | It spreads easily to others | It is a sign of being dirty at home | It looks funny | It makes the other children itch by looking
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the transmission of head lice? | They are transmitted by direct contact between heads | They are transmitted by flight between hosts | They arise from inadequate personal hygiene of the host | They are transmitted by leaping across considerable distances
exam: Identify the organism responsible for the condition commonly termed ringworm. | A fungus infecting the skin | A parasitic worm beneath the skin | A bacterium causing yellow crusts | A virus lying dormant in the nerves
exam: It is given that a person has previously had chickenpox. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The virus may later reactivate in the form of shingles | The virus is wholly eliminated from the body | The person shall contract chickenpox annually thereafter | The person is rendered immune to all viruses
exam: State the period during which a child with chickenpox ought to remain absent from school. | Until all the spots have crusted over | Until the first spot has appeared | For one day irrespective of the spots | For no period whatsoever
